{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxqAA1AFqw1kYR3k8CTL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four steps are done. The rule read back as enabled with status \"ok\".\n\n- **Data view:** \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" has the time field `timestamp`. The response code is a string field, `response` (with a `response.keyword` subfield).\n- **Dashboard:** \"S4-5af12cea Web logs\" has two panels. \"Requests over time\" is a line chart of request count by `timestamp`. \"Requests by response code\" is a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`.\n- **Alert rule:** \"S4-5af12cea 5xx errors\" is an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute over `kibana_sample_data_logs` and fires when more than 10 requests with `response.keyword` from \"500\" to \"599\" arrive in 5 minutes. Because the codes are stored as strings, the range compares them as text. That covers every 5xx code, since they are all three digits. The rule was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n- **Panel edit:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\". I didn't re-read the dashboard after the edit, so the new title is unconfirmed beyond the update call returning without error.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":586,"cache_read_input_tokens":20766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":586},"output_tokens":405,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-58d9-b388-fca8d0eba393","uuid":"e7557d39-f89d-4229-bf47-f93f09a7e9a4","timestamp":"2026-10-12T18:39:41.098Z","request_id":"req_011CfxqA9n1piy52Yga5vdYH"}
